<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Congress of Berlin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Treaty of Berlin (1878) · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War (1877–1878)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> was a meeting of the great powers chaired by Bismarck in June and July 1878 to revise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-san-stefano">Treaty of San Stefano</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a>. It recognised the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, creating Eastern Rumelia, and let <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> occupy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin.jpg" alt="Congress of Berlin" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anton von Werner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Congress_of_Berlin,_13_July_1878,_by_Anton_von_Werner.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-san-stefano">Treaty of San Stefano</a> of March 1878, which ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1877-1878, created a large Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> as an autonomous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the sultan. This extended Russian influence over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and alarmed the other powers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> saw the entire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> as its sphere of influence, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> wanted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> control over the Balkans. Otto von Bismarck therefore called the Congress and presided over it as an impartial mediator, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> stood to gain nothing from the crisis. Before it opened, Britain concluded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus-convention">Cyprus Convention</a> on 4 June 1878, a secret alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> that let Britain occupy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Congress met from 13 June to 13 July 1878 at Bismarck's Reich Chancellery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. Britain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> were members. Delegates from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> attended only the sessions that concerned their states. The Congress revised or eliminated 18 of the 29 articles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-san-stefano">Treaty of San Stefano</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> were recognised as independent. Romania received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-dobruja">Northern Dobruja</a> but had to give Southern Bessarabia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Bulgaria was divided into the Principality of Bulgaria, with nominal autonomy under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and Eastern Rumelia, an autonomous province under a Christian ruler. Macedonia stayed under the Ottoman Empire. Austria-Hungary was given the administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> and the right to garrison the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandzak">Sanjak of Novi Pazar</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> took over the administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The result was at first hailed as a success for peace, but most participants were dissatisfied. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, which had won the war, felt humiliated, and Gorchakov called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-berlin-1889">Treaty of Berlin</a> the darkest page of his life. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> was left with less than half the territory planned at San Stefano. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> resented Russia's consent to the Austrian administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, and Bismarck was hated by Russian nationalists. The League of the Three Emperors was destroyed. Historians see the settlement as having sown the seeds of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> of 1912 and 1913 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Congress of Berlin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The principality, under Ottoman suzerainty, gained de facto independence when the last Ottoman garrisons left in 1867, and the <b>Congress of Berlin</b> in 1878 recognised its formal independence and added the districts of Nišava, Pirot, Toplica and Vranje.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Congress of Berlin</b> recognised the independence of Serbia, Montenegro and Romania and cut back the large Bulgaria created by Russia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-13">13 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The other great powers revised it at the <b>Congress of Berlin</b> that summer, leaving lasting Balkan grievances.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-3">3 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After uprisings in Herzegovina and Bosnia from 1875 and the Russo-Turkish War, the <b>Congress of Berlin</b> in 1878 gave Austria-Hungary a mandate to occupy and administer Bosnia and Herzegovina, which remained nominally Ottoman.</blockquote><a href="/state/bosnia-and-herzegovina-austro-hungarian">Bosnia and Herzegovina (Austro-Hungarian)</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of San Stefano in 1878 had created a large Bulgaria, but the <b>Congress of Berlin</b> split it, leaving the southern part as Eastern Rumelia, an autonomous province of the Ottoman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-rumelia">Eastern Rumelia</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Congress of Berlin</b> reduced it to an autonomous principality between the Danube and the Balkan mountains, under Ottoman suzerainty.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-bulgaria">Principality of Bulgaria</a></li></ul></section>
